The attached image illustrates the local inflammatory response at injection sites two weeks post-immunization with either aluminum hydroxide (AH) adjuvant or Nano-11 adjuvant, as shown in Figure 11. The left panel (AH) displays extensive granulomatous inflammation within the skeletal muscle, marked by dense aggregates of inflammatory cells. In contrast, the right panel (Nano-11) shows significantly less residual inflammation, with only a few macrophages arranged in slender cords between muscle fibers. This demonstrates that Nano-11 induces a more transient and less severe inflammatory response compared to aluminum hydroxide, contributing to its favorable safety profile as a vaccine adjuvant.